Esta PR ajusta los timeouts de los tests E2E full-stack de Sport para evitar fallos intermitentes cuando la carga inicial de la pantalla tarda más de lo esperado.

## Problema

El helper `waitForSportsPage()` podía esperar hasta `30000ms` a que desapareciera el mensaje de carga:

```ts
await expect(page.getByText('Cargando deportes...')).toBeHidden({ timeout: 30000 });
```

Sin embargo, Playwright corta cada test a los `30000ms` por defecto.

Esto generaba un problema: si la carga inicial consumía casi todo el tiempo disponible del test, el caso llegaba al click en `Agregar Deporte` con muy poco tiempo restante y podía fallar por timeout, aunque la pantalla hubiera terminado cargando correctamente.

## Cambios realizados

* Se aumentó el timeout de los tests dentro del bloque `Sports Full-Stack E2E`:

```ts
test.describe('Sports Full-Stack E2E', () => {
  test.setTimeout(60_000);
});
```

* Se reforzó el helper `openCreateDialog()` para esperar explícitamente a que el botón `Agregar Deporte` esté habilitado antes de hacer click:

```ts
async function openCreateDialog(page: Page) {
  const addSportButton = page.getByRole('button', { name: /Agregar Deporte/i });
  await expect(addSportButton).toBeEnabled({ timeout: 10000 });
  await addSportButton.click();
  await expect(page.getByText('Agregar Nuevo Deporte')).toBeVisible();
}
```

## Resultado

Los tests E2E full-stack de Sport quedan más estables frente a cargas iniciales lentas. El timeout global del test ya no compite con la espera interna de carga, y el click sobre `Agregar Deporte` se realiza solo cuando el botón está efectivamente habilitado

The game dashboard was optimized for desktop and did not adapt well to phone aspect ratios, causing poor composition and clipped chat input in mobile views. This update makes layout behavior viewport- and orientation-aware so image/chat composition remains usable across desktop, portrait, and landscape.

- **Responsive layout strategy**
  - Kept desktop behavior as a horizontal split.
  - Added portrait-phone breakpoint to stack regions vertically (image top, chat/input bottom).
  - Added landscape-phone tuning to keep side-by-side layout with tighter spacing.

- **Flex sizing fixes for mobile clipping**
  - Replaced rigid `vw/vh`-bound sizing with flexible `%`/`dvh` sizing on root/dashboard containers.
  - Added `min-height: 0`/`min-width: 0` to flex children so chat can shrink correctly instead of pushing input offscreen.
  - Converted footer from fixed viewport height to content-driven sizing with explicit minimum heights.

- **Input/footer visibility hardening**
  - Added safe-area-aware bottom padding (`env(safe-area-inset-bottom)`) to prevent controls from being obscured on mobile devices with insets.
  - Reduced padding/control height in landscape-mobile mode to preserve usable chat area.

```css
@media (max-width: 900px) and (orientation: portrait) {
  #dashboard { flex-direction: column; }
  #image-canvas-container {
    flex: 0 0 45%;
    min-height: 35%;
    border-right: none;
    border-bottom: 2px solid #333;
  }
}
```

## Summary

Reads the \`?themeMode=\` URL parameter on load and uses it as \`initialMode\` for \`ThemeController\` in \`EmbeddedContextProviders\`, so embedded dashboards can start in dark/system mode without a visible light-to-dark flash.

Closes #40759

## Changes

**\`src/embedded/getInitialThemeMode.ts\`** — new module, no heavy dependencies:

\`\`\`typescript
export function getInitialThemeMode(): ThemeMode {
  const params = new URLSearchParams(window.location.search);
  const themeMode = params.get('themeMode');
  if (themeMode === 'dark') return ThemeMode.DARK;
  if (themeMode === 'system') return ThemeMode.SYSTEM;
  return ThemeMode.DEFAULT;
}
\`\`\`

Extracted into its own file so it can be tested directly without pulling in \`EmbeddedContextProviders\`'s heavy dependencies (e.g. \`query-string\` ESM).

**\`EmbeddedContextProviders.tsx\`** — imports and calls \`getInitialThemeMode()\` instead of hardcoded \`ThemeMode.DEFAULT\`.

**\`getInitialThemeMode.test.ts\`** — 5 test cases using \`jest.spyOn(window, 'location', 'get')\` (matching the codebase pattern):
- \`?themeMode=dark\` → \`ThemeMode.DARK\`
- \`?themeMode=system\` → \`ThemeMode.SYSTEM\`
- \`?themeMode=light\` → \`ThemeMode.DEFAULT\` (fallback)
- no param → \`ThemeMode.DEFAULT\`
- unrecognised value → \`ThemeMode.DEFAULT\`

**\`superset-embedded-sdk/README.md\`** — added "Setting the Initial Theme Mode" section documenting the \`themeMode\` URL parameter.

## No SDK changes required

The embed SDK already supports passing arbitrary URL params to the iframe via \`dashboardUiConfig.urlParams\`. Host apps can use this today:

\`\`\`typescript
embedDashboard({
  ...
  dashboardUiConfig: {
    urlParams: { themeMode: 'dark' },
  },
});
\`\`\`

## Backward compatibility

- No URL param → \`ThemeMode.DEFAULT\` (unchanged behaviour)
- \`ThemeMode.SYSTEM\` has an existing fallback guard in \`isValidThemeMode\` — no additional handling needed

Zero-risk project docs (no app code) — the chores that pair well with waiting on a deploy.

## What's here

- **`PRODUCT.md`** + **`DESIGN.md`** — the design-context files the `impeccable` skill reads before any design work. They capture the conventions this codebase already follows so they don't get re-derived (or drifted from) each time:
  - the calm / anti-shame product thesis and anti-references (no gamification, no fitness-dashboard look, no red on a missed day);
  - monochrome structure with **one category accent at a time**;
  - the **44px `tapTarget`** convention (`src/lib/ui.ts`);
  - **"shape follows job"** — chips = schedule, rail = quota, heatmap = history, rows = editing;
  - the hairline-divider + uppercase-header **section rhythm**;
  - **partners as the trust boundary**.

  `DESIGN.md` follows the Google Stitch format (token frontmatter + the six fixed sections); `register: product`. Both verified loading via the skill's context loader (`hasProduct/hasDesign: true`).

- **`docs/smoke-checklist.md`** — a manual runbook over the core flows for after a deploy, and a fuller pass before letting an outsider in (the partner flows that need a 2nd account, the weekly-email cron, and the **RLS negative check** with a non-partner account).

## Not included

- The **Next dependency patch** (PostCSS advisory) — that's a real code/lockfile change, so it stays its own careful PR (never `--force`), ideally on a clean base.
- The `DESIGN.json` sidecar (for the Stitch live panel) — optional; can be generated later with `$impeccable document` if you ever use the panel.

