## What was found

Continued systematic audit of every user-facing route file. This pass targeted the hottest endpoints in `suppliers.js`, `packages.js`, `reviews.js`, and `tickets.js`.

---

## Missing `id` unique indexes on all hot collections

Four high-traffic collections had no `id` index. Every `findOne({ id })` call introduced by earlier PRs was still doing a server-side scan:

| Collection | Indexes added |
|---|---|
| `suppliers` | `{ id: 1 }` unique + `{ approved, category }` compound |
| `packages` | `{ id: 1 }` unique + `{ supplierId, approved }` compound |
| `reviews` | `{ id: 1 }` unique |
| `tickets` | `{ id: 1 }` unique |

---

## Full scans eliminated in hot user-facing routes

### `GET /suppliers/:id/packages` (every supplier profile page)
```js
// BEFORE: two full collection scans
const supplier = (await dbUnified.read('suppliers')).find(x => x.id === id && x.approved);
const pkgs = (await dbUnified.read('packages')).filter(p => p.supplierId === id && p.approved);

// AFTER: two targeted indexed queries
const supplier = await dbUnified.findOne('suppliers', { id, approved: true });
const pkgs = await dbUnified.find('packages', { supplierId: id, approved: true });
```

### `GET /me/suppliers` (every supplier dashboard load)
```js
// BEFORE
(await dbUnified.read('suppliers')).filter(s => s.ownerUserId === req.user.id)
// AFTER
await dbUnified.find('suppliers', { ownerUserId: req.user.id })
```

### `GET /me/packages` (every supplier dashboard — was a double full scan)
```js
// BEFORE: read all suppliers, filter, then read all packages, filter
const mine = (await dbUnified.read('suppliers')).filter(ownerUserId).map(s => s.id);
const items = (await dbUnified.read('packages')).filter(p => mine.includes(p.supplierId));

// AFTER: two targeted queries using $in
const mySuppliers = await dbUnified.find('suppliers', { ownerUserId: req.user.id });
const items = await dbUnified.find('packages', { supplierId: { $in: supplierIds } });
```

### `reviews.js` (both review-by-id lookups)
```js
// BEFORE
const reviews = await dbUnified.read('reviews');
const review = reviews.find(r => r.id === reviewId);
// AFTER
const review = await dbUnified.findOne('reviews', { id: reviewId });
```

---

## Dead code removed from `POST /api/tickets`

The POST handler was fetching all of the user's tickets at the start, pushing the new ticket onto the array, then calling `insertOne`. The array was never written back (we use `insertOne` now). This was a leftover from when the handler used `write()` — the fetch and push were dead code on every ticket creation:

```js
// REMOVED — fetched only to push to, never written back
const tickets = (await dbUnified.find('tickets', { userId })).map(...);
...
tickets.push(newTicket);
await dbUnified.insertOne('tickets', newTicket); // only this line matters
```

Also: admin notification lookup changed from `read('users').filter(role === 'admin')` → `find('users', { role: 'admin' })`.

